"""BEHAVIOR-specific motion guidance; does not alter actions or native physics."""

MOTION_INSTRUCTIONS = '''
BEHAVIOR motion discipline (applies to ordinary tools and coding_control):
An absolute EEF target is sent to IK immediately. steps=30 means repeat that SAME target for 30 control steps; it does NOT interpolate a path over one second or limit speed. A long segment, or a longer coding_control program, does not make a large target jump gentle. IK joint-angle bounds are not trajectory planning or a guarantee of smooth motion. Stay within the conservative prompt limits below even though the schema permits a larger workspace; these are policy instructions, not additional runtime-enforced clamps.

For ordinary move_arms / move_robot calls:
- Start from the latest MEASURED eef_left/right_pos and quat, not your previous requested pose. Limit each arm's target translation to 0.03 m Euclidean distance from that measurement (not 0.03 m per axis). Near objects, during grasp/release/insertion, or when uncertain, use at most 0.005 m.
- Limit the shortest relative orientation change to 5 degrees per call; near contact use at most 2 degrees. Keep orientation unchanged unless needed. Compute intermediate unit XYZW quaternions along the shortest rotation; q and -q represent the same orientation. Never jump directly to a distant canonical downward pose. Translate and reorient in separate phases when reachability is uncertain.
- Use 10..30 steps to let a SMALL target settle; shortening steps does not make a distant target safe. Re-read measured progress after each segment. Before advancing another waypoint, require position residual <=0.01 m in free space (<=0.003 m for precision work) and orientation residual <=3 degrees where orientation was commanded. If not converged, do not add another displacement to the previous target: reduce the offset from the new measurement. If progress stalls across two feedback segments or oscillates, stop advancing, inspect observations, and revise the approach or retreat in small increments. These tolerances are feedback checks, not proof of task completion.
- Limit each commanded trunk joint change to 0.02 rad from its latest measured value, and respect its joint limits. Initially adjust the trunk with the base stopped and arm targets near measured poses; then remeasure before extending the arms. Do not combine a large trunk/arm/base reconfiguration in one command. Small coordinated motions remain available.
- Limit base planar speed magnitude to 0.10 m/s and |yaw speed| to 0.20 rad/s; near furniture or while manipulating, at most 0.03 m/s and 0.08 rad/s, or stop. Inspect onboard RGB-D before advancing. These conservative speeds do not guarantee clearance.

For coding_control, when enabled, implement the gradual reference trajectory yourself:
- A callback gets one NEW 30Hz measurement, but there is NO automatic smoothing. Use dt to limit reference translation speed to 0.06 m/s (0.002 m/tick), or 0.015 m/s (0.0005 m/tick) near contact. Limit orientation reference speed to 15 degrees/s, or 6 degrees/s near contact; trunk reference speed to 0.05 rad/s per joint. Initialize references from the current measured pose. Apply the base speed limits above and ramp base velocity changes instead of abrupt reversals.
- Keep target-to-measurement error within the ordinary-tool position/orientation limits above as well as bounding consecutive reference changes. Do not blindly integrate last_targets or elapsed time while the arm is blocked. Pause reference advancement when tracking lags; if inherited targets are already far from the measurement, end the segment with done and inspect before continuing. Do not generate a fresh large jump to catch up.
- Persistent targets include omitted EEF/trunk components: continuing to omit a bad target still commands it. Check both arms and trunk, including components you are holding. Return done for fresh visual reasoning if tracking oscillates, depth becomes unreliable, or a planned phase needs scene interpretation. Only the evaluator determines task success.

The aim is deliberate, feedback-guided motion. Do not trade a shorter action-call count for large jumps. Continue trying revised small motions while the episode remains active.
'''
